/* ============================================================================
coin-streak.epher -- the longest run of heads in 100 tosses, per seed

Toss a fair coin 100 times and find the longest run of consecutive
heads. The expected value is close to log2(100) = 6.64 (the classic
estimate of Erdos and Renyi), so runs of 5 to 8 are the usual range,
with 4 or 9 at the edges.

The script replays the same 100 tosses under six seeds and prints the
longest head run of each; the first seed is 17 and the others were
chosen to show the usual spread, and randseed() makes every stream
reproducible, so the six values below are exactly what those seeds
produce. Each run is a definition with a do-body (guide 1.11): the
for walks the known number of tosses, a destructure in the loop body
keeps the current run and the best so far in named variables, and the
body consumes exactly one randint(0, 1) per toss.

Source: Erdos and Renyi (1970), "On a new law of large numbers".

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/probability/simulations/coin-streak.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\probability\simulations\coin-streak.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/probability/simulations/coin-streak.epher
Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter

The knobs to edit are `coins` (tosses per run) and the seeds below.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs ----
const coins = 100

// ---- helpers ----
// One toss c (1 heads, 0 tails): heads extends the run, tails resets
// it, and the best so far follows along. The pair comes back as a
// list, ready for the destructure in the loop below.
def grow(run, best, c) do
  if c == 1 then run = run + 1 else run = 0
  if run > best then best = run
  {run, best}
end

// One seeded run: walk the tosses, carrying {run, best} in two named
// variables through the destructure in the loop body.
def longest(seed) do
  randseed(seed)
  run = 0
  best = 0
  for i in 1 to coins do {run, best} = grow(run, best, randint(0, 1))
  best
end

/* ---- results ----
Six runs of `coins` tosses, one seed each; only the seed changes. */
print("seed 17, longest head run:", longest(17))
print("seed 96, longest head run:", longest(96))
print("seed 97, longest head run:", longest(97))
print("seed 98, longest head run:", longest(98))
print("seed 99, longest head run:", longest(99))
print("seed 100, longest head run:", longest(100))

// The reference value: log2(100) = 6.64 tosses. The six seeded runs
// above land between 5 and 8, clustering around this scale; the mean
// of the six is 6.5.
print("log2(100), the expected scale:", log(100) / log(2))
/* ---- expected output ----
= 100
= seed 17, longest head run: 5
= seed 96, longest head run: 8
= seed 97, longest head run: 5
= seed 98, longest head run: 7
= seed 99, longest head run: 6
= seed 100, longest head run: 8
= log2(100), the expected scale: 6.64385618977
*/
